From: DoM!niC <info@dom1nic.eu>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: hamoa: give gpucc its MX and CX power domains

gpucc has no power domains, so the GPU's GDSCs (GPU_CX_GDSC, GPU_GX_GDSC)
have no parent and the level the GMU requests on "cx" reaches no rail. This
goes unnoticed while rpmhpd holds all rails at their highest level, which it
does until every consumer in the devicetree has probed (sync_state). With
the video clock controller built (CONFIG_SM_VIDEOCC_8550) that point is
reached, the rails drop to what is actually requested, and depending on
timing the GMU stops answering on the Surface Laptop 7:

  a6xx_hfi_wait_for_msg_interrupt: Message HFI_H2F_MSG_GX_BW_PERF_VOTE
    timed out waiting for response
  hangcheck detected gpu lockup rb 0!

Describe MX and CX as the binding (qcom,sm8450-gpucc.yaml) provides for;
the clock driver then makes the GDSCs subdomains of both rails.
---
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi
index 8a96ea1..61c1bda 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/hamoa.dtsi
@@ -4204,6 +4204,10 @@ gpucc: clock-controller@3d90000 {
 			clocks = <&bi_tcxo_div2>,
 				 <&gcc GCC_GPU_GPLL0_CPH_CLK_SRC>,
 				 <&gcc GCC_GPU_GPLL0_DIV_CPH_CLK_SRC>;
+			power-domains = <&rpmhpd RPMHPD_MX>,
+					<&rpmhpd RPMHPD_CX>;
+			required-opps = <&rpmhpd_opp_low_svs>,
+					<&rpmhpd_opp_low_svs>;
 			#clock-cells = <1>;
 			#reset-cells = <1>;
 			#power-domain-cells = <1>;
